{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes a spin-wheel reward mechanic in which the wheel appears to stop on a valuable prize, but the game then forces or triggers another spin and replaces the apparent prize with a lower one. This explicitly alleges that the game manipulates the determination of the outcome after the player sees a favorable result, supporting Illusion of Control. The wheel also delivers unpredictable rewards through repeated spins, which supports Reward Mania. No real-money stake is described, so Gambling is not assigned.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "the wheel spin after stopping on a great price the genie move the wheel to a spin tag, and then the prices after the spin are way lower than the original price",
      "rule_applied": "vs Alleged rigging",
      "rationale": "The span alleges that the game overrides an apparent favorable outcome and manipulates the result to produce a lower prize."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_RewardMania",
      "span": "the wheel spin",
      "rule_applied": "Gacha mechanics like pulls, rolls, banners; Variable Rewards",
      "rationale": "A spin wheel is a chance-based reward-delivery mechanism whose repeated and variable outcomes sustain engagement."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a matchmaking wait of 30 seconds before a match begins, but this is a service or queue delay rather than an in-game timer that schedules or gates play, so it does not satisfy Playing by Appointment. It also describes matches and battling to win, which establishes player-versus-player competition. The difficulty of obtaining weapon upgrades and perks is stated as a general difficulty, without a repeated task, payment requirement, or specific reward-delivery mechanic, so it does not support additional labels.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "connect to a match",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "Connecting to matches and battling to win describes a game structure in which players compete against one another."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes competitive multiplayer against other players, including repeated encounters with specific cards and difficulty defending against them. It explicitly characterizes the game as pay to win and says advancing in rank is not achievable through skill alone, implying that spending provides an advantage. The complaint that cards are buffed in updates does not establish Power Creep because the reviewer does not say that a purchased card lost value after acquisition. Using annoying strategies to win is ordinary competitive play and does not show incentivized dishonest or harmful behavior. No time, reward, collection, advertising, or other coded mechanic is described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "all you play against after 8k trophies is mega knight,pekka, and golem",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The span explicitly describes the player competing against other players using specific cards."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "its also pay to win and usually doesn't let you get higher from just using skill",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win indicator under M_PayToProgress",
      "rationale": "The review states that spending money provides an advantage and that progressing to a higher rank cannot normally be achieved through skill alone."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a paid pass as a purchase option, which supports the battle-pass indicator for Recurring Fee. It also explicitly describes friends and team members trading lives, a resource exchange that supports Reciprocity. The reference to 9 diamonds does not establish real money being converted into currency, and the missing game element is presented as a customer-service issue rather than a deliberate design mechanic. The move counts describe gameplay conditions, not a real-time wait or appointment.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_RecurringFee",
      "span": "you don't buy from this game beyond the pass",
      "rule_applied": "Battle passes [indicators]",
      "rationale": "The review identifies a pass as a purchase and discusses buying beyond it, which describes a paid battle-pass mechanic."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Reciprocity",
      "span": "Get friends and a team and trade lives.",
      "rule_applied": "Feeling obligated to return a favor, share resources, trade an item, etc.",
      "rationale": "Trading lives is an explicit exchange of in-game resources with other players."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes two existing time-based mechanics despite phrasing them as suggestions. Lives take longer than the desired 5–10 minutes to become available, implying a regeneration timer that delays play. Daily prizes reset when the player misses a day, which is a daily-return incentive with an explicit missed-day penalty. The increasing difficulty of levels and general praise do not by themselves establish grinding or another coded mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "maybe only 5-10 minutes for a new life",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play: in-game timers that impose a wait before gameplay can continue.",
      "rationale": "The request for a shorter time for a new life implies that life regeneration currently imposes a real-time wait before the player can continue."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_DailyRewards",
      "span": "just because you miss a day doesnt mean that you need to start over on your daily prizes",
      "rule_applied": "Daily Rewards: daily login incentives with an active consequence for missing a day, such as resetting the reward sequence.",
      "rationale": "Missing a day resets the player's daily-prize progress, explicitly describing a daily reward penalty."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an in-game advertisement that cannot be dismissed normally. There is no close control, tapping the screen redirects the player to the store, and returning to the game leaves the advertisement screen active until the app is forcibly terminated. This indicates compelled exposure to an in-game ad and an interface that obstructs opting out while steering taps toward the store. The review does not explicitly attribute the behavior to a bug or technical fault.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "after seeing the ads, there is no way to close it",
      "rule_applied": "Forced to watch ads",
      "rationale": "The player cannot dismiss the in-game advertisement and must remain on the ad screen or terminate the game."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "No cross mark on the screen , if you try to click anywhere on the screen it takes you to store",
      "rule_applied": "Opt-out obstruction: an absent close control and taps routed toward a purchase/store destination",
      "rationale": "The interface removes the normal opt-out control and redirects attempted dismissal taps to the store."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes accumulated in-game purchases and progress becoming a reason not to delete the game, because uninstalling would cause those investments to be lost. This makes leaving costly and creates reluctance to abandon the game. The requested save feature is hypothetical, but the existing loss-on-deletion behavior is explicitly described and can be coded.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_EasyToGetHardToLose",
      "span": "if I deleted the game I would lose everything my in-game purchases and my hard work I really don't want that to happen",
      "rule_applied": "Invested (Endowed) Value: accumulated time, money, and progress make it difficult to abandon the game; exit entails forfeiting value already held.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer is reluctant to leave because deleting the game would forfeit purchased items and accumulated progress."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

