{
  "analysis": "The review describes an energy system that limits continued play. Once the player runs out of energy, further progress is blocked until a recovery timer restores it, imposing an in-game wait and requiring the player to return according to the timer rather than their own preferred schedule. No payment alternative is mentioned, and the requests for console play, a storyline, bigger maps, and customization are hypothetical suggestions rather than existing mechanics.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "It's an Energy based game so once you run out you can't really progress any further, and the timer is fairly long to recover fully.",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play: in-game timers that impose arbitrary waiting, including energy regeneration, and the boundary rule requiring the span to describe the wait or regeneration schedule.",
      "rationale": "Running out of energy blocks progress until a long recovery timer refills it, forcing play around the game's regeneration schedule."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a capture mechanic in which Poké Balls are consumed when Pokémon break out, and the reviewer believes that after purchasing Poké Balls, breakouts happen more often, forcing additional purchases. This supports Pay to Progress because continued capturing is presented as requiring more purchased resources, and Illusion of Control because the reviewer alleges that the game manipulates capture outcomes after purchase. The final edit states that the review itself was not written by the account holder and that the claimed conversations with other players did not occur, implying an unauthorized review posted through the player's identity, which supports Friend Spam / Impersonation. The review does not describe real money being converted into currency, a chance-based monetary wager, or a reward-delivery system.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "so I go through the balls faster so I have to purchase more",
      "rule_applied": "definition; payment is described as necessary to maintain continued capture activity after the consumable resource is depleted",
      "rationale": "The reviewer says the game consumes Poké Balls faster and thereby makes further purchases necessary to continue capturing."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "since I started purchasing, items \"poke\" balls that when I tried to make a capture that they keep breaking out",
      "rule_applied": "Alleged rigging boundary rule",
      "rationale": "The reviewer alleges that capture outcomes became manipulated after purchasing Poké Balls, asserting that the game determines results in a concealed or unfair way."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_FriendSpamImpersonation",
      "span": "I DID NOT WRITE THIS REVIEW.....I DIDN'T TALK TO SEVERAL PLAYERS BECAUSE I DO NOT KNOW ANYONE",
      "rule_applied": "Destination of the message rule; unauthorized content published as the player",
      "rationale": "The edit says the review and its claims were not authored by the account holder, implying that content was posted through the player's identity without consent."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes two mechanics. First, hard levels can take a week to pass, but this is only a statement of slow or difficult progress and does not identify a repetitive task, an in-game timer, or a required play schedule, so it does not support a label. Second, some tasks are described as impossible unless the player buys boosters, making payment the route past an obstacle and supporting Pay to Progress. No web search is needed.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "there are way too many impossible tasks unless you buy boosters",
      "rule_applied": "Pay Wall / Free alternative boundary rule",
      "rationale": "The review states that continued progress through some tasks is impossible without purchasing boosters."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a referral mechanic that rewards inviting friends and getting them to join Facebook, so the game uses social recruitment as an incentive. It also describes spending or staking accumulated dice through a maximum-bet play that produces chance-based outcomes such as Shut Downs and Bank Heists, with the reviewer receiving none, which supports Gambling. The repeated duplicate stickers describe an unpredictable reward pool delivering unwanted repeats, supporting Reward Mania. The inability to log in is explicitly attributed to an internet connection error, so it is a technical fault and does not support a dark-pattern label. No real-money purchase of dice is described, so Intermediate Currency and Pay to Progress are not assigned.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_ForcedFellowship",
      "span": "no rewards for inviting friends and getting them to join facebook",
      "rule_applied": "Social Pyramid Scheme indicator",
      "rationale": "The game ties rewards to recruiting friends and bringing them into the game through Facebook."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_Gambling",
      "span": "when I finally had more than 500 dices and played max bet, absolutely NO LUCK! No Shut downs and no bank heists",
      "rule_applied": "Gambling definition; chance-based play using an in-game stake qualifies even without a real-money purchase being described",
      "rationale": "The reviewer describes staking dice on maximum-bet play for uncertain outcomes that may produce Shut Downs or Bank Heists."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_RewardMania",
      "span": "Stickers are duplicates all the way",
      "rule_applied": "Variable Rewards indicator; repeated unwanted drops from a randomized reward pool",
      "rationale": "The sticker reward system repeatedly delivers duplicate items rather than predictable desired rewards."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an in-game interface that automatically scrolls to and selects the most expensive available 1v1 betting table, rather than leaving the player at a neutral or low-cost choice. This is UI Misdirection because the interface directs the player toward a costly option and creates accidental mis-click risk. The table entry uses coins, and the reviewer says losing those coins forces players to buy more with real money, which supports both Intermediate Currency and Pay to Progress. The mention of the 1v1 section also describes direct competition between players. The review does not establish a chance-based wager, so Gambling is not assigned.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "it automatically scrolls and selects the highest possible bet table based on your coins",
      "rule_applied": "Exciting UI Effect / attention-directing interface; the UI directs the player toward a selected costly option and away from a safer opt-out choice.",
      "rationale": "The interface automatically places attention and selection on the highest-cost table, creating the described accidental mis-click risk."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_IntermediateCurrency",
      "span": "players lose all their hard-earned coins and are forced to buy real ones",
      "rule_applied": "Intermediate Currency definition: real-world money is used to acquire in-game currency.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer describes coins as the in-game currency and says players must buy more of them with real money."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "players lose all their hard-earned coins and are forced to buy real ones",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Progress definition and Free Alternative boundary: payment is presented as necessary after the in-game currency is lost in order to continue playing.",
      "rationale": "After the interface causes players to lose their coins, the review says they are forced to pay for replacement coins to keep playing."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "go to the 1v1 section",
      "rule_applied": "Competition definition: the game makes players compete against other players.",
      "rationale": "A 1v1 section explicitly describes player-versus-player competition."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a perceived outcome-manipulation mechanic: the RNG is said to be against the player, which asserts that results are being determined unfairly rather than merely reporting bad luck. The review also describes an unfavorable resource economy at higher levels: a level costs 2200 and a win barely returns that amount, while spending actual money is portrayed as the alternative. However, it does not explicitly say that payment is required to continue or progress, only that playing without spending is not rewarding, so Pay to Progress is not assigned. The weak return also does not show stranded currency or a concrete shortfall below an item cost, so Waste Aversion is not assigned.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "the rng is so obviously against you",
      "rule_applied": "Alleged rigging boundary rule",
      "rationale": "The reviewer asserts that the game’s random outcomes are manipulated against the player, which describes hidden or unfair outcome determination."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a base-building game as repetitive, but “same old same old grinder” is a general quality characterization without a named repeated activity tied to progress, so it does not support Grinding. The review explicitly describes winning events as requiring substantial spending, using “pay to play,” “pay to win,” and a reported 30k expenditure to win the last event. This is a monetary progression/competitive-advantage mechanic. It does not clearly state that the event involves competing against other players, so Competition is not assigned.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "Zero skill pay to win. Calculated total spent on last event was 30k to win.",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win indicator under M_PayToProgress",
      "rationale": "The span states that spending money is required to gain the advantage needed to win an event."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes several current game mechanics. Spending money provides competitive advantage, explicitly described as pay to win. Base upgrades use increasingly long update timers, imposing waits on continued development. The game is described as endlessly scaling with no meaningful endpoint, supporting an infinite treadmill rather than merely slow progress; the phrase \"more grind\" does not identify a repeated activity clearly enough for Grinding. The UI is said to be deliberately confusing in order to push purchases, which is UI Misdirection. Alliances and attacks create direct player-versus-player conflict, supporting Competition. The review does not describe rewards for inviting friends, an obligation to teammates, a concrete purchase prompt, or harmful behavior that gives aggressors a stated reward.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "it's set up in a pay to win way",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win indicator under M_PayToProgress",
      "rationale": "The reviewer explicitly states that spending money gives players an in-game advantage."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "the update times for your base are becoming ridiculous",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play: in-game timers that impose arbitrary waits",
      "rationale": "Base development is governed by update timers that delay continued progress."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_InfiniteTreadmill",
      "span": "Everything scales endlessly, there is kinda no point to look forward to",
      "rule_applied": "Infinite Treadmill definition: the game extends without an attainable end state",
      "rationale": "The review describes endlessly scaling content and no meaningful completion or endpoint."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "The UI is deliberately confusing and overwhelming to achieve the effect that you need to buy stuff",
      "rule_applied": "UI Misdirection boundary: a confusing interface designed to steer players toward purchases",
      "rationale": "The interface is explicitly described as confusing by design to direct players toward buying."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "If you are not in one then you get attacked and bullied",
      "rule_applied": "Competition definition: the game makes players compete against other players",
      "rationale": "Players are subject to attacks from other players and alliances, establishing direct competitive conflict."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a daily-reward system that has been changed so the player cannot claim the daily rewards without first betting 50,000 in-game units. This directly identifies Daily Rewards because access to the reward is conditional on completing a daily requirement. The phrase \"bet 50,000\" also describes a wager in a casino-style game; the stake appears to be in-game currency, and the codebook allows Gambling when the stake is earned in-game without a real-money purchase being stated. The timed rewards and their 40,000 maximum describe reward amounts, but not a sufficiently specific unpredictable delivery mechanic for Reward Mania. The review does not establish a real-world waiting gate, payment requirement, or progress barrier.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_DailyRewards",
      "span": "I can't get my daily rewards unless I bet 50,000 with out it I can't complete my daily rewards",
      "rule_applied": "definition; daily reward mechanism with an active requirement or consequence",
      "rationale": "The player must complete a daily betting requirement to obtain or complete the daily rewards."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_Gambling",
      "span": "unless I bet 50,000",
      "rule_applied": "Gambling vs Reward Mania boundary; a wager using in-game currency is sufficient even when real-money purchase of the stake is not described",
      "rationale": "The review explicitly describes staking 50,000 in-game units on a casino-style bet for a reward-related outcome."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

