    {
      "label": "M_IntermediateCurrency",
      "span": "I have also paid a lot for extra coins and things to see if pin pulls would happen",
      "rule_applied": "Intermediate Currency definition: real-world money is used to acquire in-game currency for use within the game world.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer explicitly says they paid real money for extra coins, describing the conversion of money into virtual currency."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes a loss-of-holdings behavior tied to leaving the game: a full bag is empty on return, and both earned and purchased coins have been consumed. This is not merely a general complaint about lost value; the span connects the loss to ending or leaving a play session. The mechanic therefore prevents the player from safely stopping and forfeits accumulated holdings when they leave, matching Mandatory Marathon. The review does not explicitly say the player is reluctant to quit or pressured to continue, so Easy to Get, Hard to Lose is not assigned separately.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_MandatoryMarathon",
      "span": "I leave the game with my bag full only to come back with it empty and coins that I earned and purchased used",
      "rule_applied": "Boundary rule: Mandatory Marathon applies where leaving a session causes accumulated holdings or progress to be forfeited; the loss is tied to stopping play rather than being described as a bug.",
      "rationale": "Leaving the game results in the player's stored items and coins being lost or consumed, so the player cannot safely stop at a moment of their choosing."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes progress and competitive viability being tied to payment: the free route is portrayed as unable to support advancement, and the player must buy the newest bot rather than earn it. The shifting meta also implies that newer bots reduce the usefulness of older ones, supporting Power Creep. On every login, purchase offers appear before gameplay, so the offers actively interrupt access to play and reduce the friction of buying. Finally, the review describes free-to-play rewards as obtainable through ads, which indicates incentivized ad viewing. The review does not clearly describe a daily reward cycle, social pressure, a chance-based purchase, or a specific fear of missing out.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "If you want to progress without paying money, goodluck!",
      "rule_applied": "Free alternative boundary rule and definition",
      "rationale": "The review states that progressing through the free route is effectively unavailable without payment."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_EasyToPurchase",
      "span": "everytime you log in, your screen has spam of so called \"good deals\" in which I mean in app purchases before you can actually play your game",
      "rule_applied": "Prompted to Buy; purchase prompts that directly intercept gameplay",
      "rationale": "In-app purchase offers appear on login and must be dealt with before the player can play."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PowerCreep",
